1. What is a Servlet?
→ A Java class that handles HTTP requests and generates HTTP responses. It runs inside a Servlet Container (e.g., Tomcat).

2. What is JSP?
→ JavaServer Pages. Used to create dynamic web pages using HTML + Java. JSP is compiled into a Servlet.

3. Servlet vs JSP
Servlet  → Business logic / Request processing
JSP      → Presentation layer (UI)

4. Servlet Lifecycle
init()      → Called once when servlet is created.
service()   → Handles every client request.
destroy()   → Called before servlet is destroyed.

5. HTTP Methods
doGet()     → Read data (GET)
doPost()    → Create data (POST)
doPut()     → Update data (PUT)
doDelete()  → Delete data (DELETE)

6. What is HttpServlet?
→ Base class used to create Servlets. Provides doGet(), doPost(), etc.

7. What is HttpServletRequest?
→ Used to read client request data.

Common Methods
request.getParameter("id")
request.getHeader("Authorization")
request.getCookies()
request.getSession()
request.getAttribute("name")
request.setAttribute("name", value)

8. What is HttpServletResponse?
→ Used to send data back to the client.

Common Methods
response.sendRedirect("/login")
response.setStatus(200)
response.getWriter().println("Hello")
response.addCookie(cookie)

9. What is HttpSession?
→ Stores user-specific data across multiple requests (e.g., login information).

10. What is ServletContext?
→ Stores application-wide shared data accessible by all Servlets.

11. What is DispatcherServlet?
→ Front Controller of Spring MVC. It receives all requests and forwards them to the appropriate Controller.

Flow
Browser
   ↓
DispatcherServlet
   ↓
Controller
   ↓
Service
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
Database

12. What is @Controller?
→ Returns a View (JSP, Thymeleaf, etc.).

13. What is @RestController?
→ Returns JSON/XML directly. Combination of @Controller + @ResponseBody.

14. Difference: @Controller vs @RestController
@Controller      → Returns View
@RestController  → Returns JSON

15. What is View Resolver?
→ Maps logical view names to JSP files.

Example
return "home";

Resolves to
/WEB-INF/views/home.jsp

16. What is Model?
→ Passes data from Controller to JSP.

Example
model.addAttribute("name", "Abhi");

JSP
${name}

17. What is ModelAndView?
→ Holds both Model data and View name.

18. What is @RequestMapping?
→ Maps a URL to a Controller method.

19. What is @GetMapping?
→ Handles HTTP GET requests.

20. What is @PostMapping?
→ Handles HTTP POST requests.

21. What is @PutMapping?
→ Handles HTTP PUT requests.

22. What is @DeleteMapping?
→ Handles HTTP DELETE requests.

23. What is @RequestParam?
→ Reads query parameters.

Example
/users?id=10

@RequestParam int id

24. What is @PathVariable?
→ Reads values from the URL path.

Example
/users/10

@PathVariable int id

25. What is @RequestBody?
→ Converts JSON request body into a Java object.

26. What is @ResponseBody?
→ Converts Java object into JSON response.

27. What is @ModelAttribute?
→ Binds form data to a Java object.

28. What is JSTL?
→ JSP Standard Tag Library used instead of Java scriptlets.

Common Tags
<c:if>
<c:forEach>
<c:choose>

29. What is Expression Language (EL)?
→ Accesses data in JSP without Java code.

Examples
${user.name}
${employee.id}
${list}

30. JSP Lifecycle
Translation → Compilation → Class Loading → Initialization → Request Processing → Destroy

31. Servlet vs Spring MVC

Servlet
- Uses doGet(), doPost()
- Manual request handling
- More boilerplate

Spring MVC
- Uses annotations
- Automatic request mapping
- Cleaner code
- Easier to maintain

